$DIR/test.p:13:34: a slice expression on a string is not permitted in Plutonium (a cut must fall between runes; use strings.Slice(s, i, j) or strings.SliceFrom(s, i))
    A string cannot be cut with a slice expression such as s[i:j]. Every string
    holds valid UTF-8 text, and a character can take up to four bytes, so a cut
    at an arbitrary byte offset could split one in two. In Go the cut works and
    leaves a string that is not valid text. Here the cut would have to panic for
    some offsets, so s[:40] would work for English text and panic on the first
    accented letter.
    Say that the offsets are positions between characters. strings.Slice(s, i,
    j) and strings.SliceFrom(s, i) take the substring and panic unless each
    bound is on a character boundary (0, len(s), or the offset of a byte that is
    not a continuation byte). The substring shares the memory of s. An offset
    from strings.Index and the other search functions is always on a boundary.
    To keep at most n bytes, use strings.Truncate(s, n), which rounds down to a
    boundary, and utf8.RuneStartBefore(s, i) to find the boundary yourself.
    Arrays and slices are still cut with a[i:j], and s[i] reads one byte.
    Go lets a program cut a string anywhere and does not check that the pieces
    are text. A Plutonium string is always valid UTF-8, and code that is valid
    Go must not compile and then fail on the first non-ASCII input. The cut has
    a name, so the check is visible in the source, and the compiler turns the
    call into the same code as a slice expression.
    To take the rest of the string, which panics unless the bound is on a rune
    boundary:
        import "pluto/strings"

        sub := strings.SliceFrom(s, 1)
    (see "Slice expressions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.12)
    (run "pluto explain E2019" for a longer explanation)
